From: FlipperEmu
Subject: [PATCH] hw/gpio/stm32l4x5_gpio: let a device release an open-drain line

stm32l4x5_gpio_set() refuses an external level on an output pin, except
level 0 on an open-drain output. So a device that shares an open-drain
line (a bus like 1-Wire or I2C, wired-AND with a pull-up) can pull it low
once but never let it go: the pin stays "connected low" and reads low
for good, even after the device has released it.

An open-drain output only ever pulls the line low, so an external level
of 1 cannot fight it; accept both levels for open-drain outputs. With ODR
1 the line then reads what the other device does, high when it lets go;
with ODR 0 it reads low as before. Push-pull outputs still refuse an
external level. Two places assumed no external high on an open-drain
output: a MODER or OTYPER write disconnected such a pin (dropping the
pull-up, so the pin floated and kept reading low), and update_gpio_idr()
had no case for an open-drain output with ODR 1 whose pin is connected
high (it took the pin as floating and kept a stale IDR). Now only
push-pull outputs are disconnected, and that case reads high.

Seen with the Flipper Zero model: the firmware reads iButton keys as a
1-Wire host on an open-drain pin, and a key (the 1-Wire slave) must pull
the line low for its presence pulse and 0 bits, then release it.

Signed-off-by: FlipperEmu
---
--- a/hw/gpio/stm32l4x5_gpio.c
+++ b/hw/gpio/stm32l4x5_gpio.c
@@ -95,11 +95,13 @@
 {
     Stm32l4x5GpioState *s = opaque;
     /*
-     * The pin isn't set if line is configured in output mode
-     * except if level is 0 and the output is open-drain.
-     * This way there will be no short-circuit prone situations.
+     * The pin isn't set if line is configured in push-pull output mode.
+     * This way there will be no short-circuit prone situations.  An
+     * open-drain output only ever pulls the line low, so another device on
+     * the line may both pull it low and let it go again (level 1: the line
+     * then reads high while ODR is 1, as with an external pull-up).
      */
-    if (is_output(s, line) && !(is_open_drain(s, line) && (level == 0))) {
+    if (is_output(s, line) && !is_open_drain(s, line)) {
         qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR, "Line %d can't be driven externally\n",
                       line);
         return;
@@ -137,6 +139,9 @@
                 /* open-drain ODR 1 with pin connected low */
                 new_idr_mask |= (1 << i);
                 new_idr &= ~(1 << i);
+            } else if (!(s->disconnected_pins & (1 << i))) {
+                /* open-drain ODR 1 with pin connected high */
+                new_idr_mask |= (1 << i);
             /* open-drain ODR 1 with unactive pin */
             } else if (is_pull_up(s, i)) {
                 new_idr_mask |= (1 << i);
@@ -196,9 +201,9 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * Return mask of pins that are both configured in output
- * mode and externally driven (except pins in open-drain
- * mode externally set to 0).
+ * Return mask of pins that are both configured in push-pull
+ * output mode and externally driven (an open-drain output
+ * shares its line: it only ever pulls it low).
  */
 static uint32_t get_gpio_pinmask_to_disconnect(Stm32l4x5GpioState *s)
 {
@@ -206,8 +211,8 @@
     for (int i = 0; i < GPIO_NUM_PINS; i++) {
         /* for each connected pin in output mode */
         if (!(s->disconnected_pins & (1 << i)) && is_output(s, i)) {
-            /* if either push-pull or high level */
-            if (is_push_pull(s, i) || s->pins_connected_high & (1 << i)) {
+            /* if push-pull */
+            if (is_push_pull(s, i)) {
                 pins_to_disconnect |= (1 << i);
                 qemu_log_mask(LOG_GUEST_ERROR,
                               "Line %d can't be driven externally\n",
